## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is the single source where all CMS content enters the React component tree. It currently freezes content via useMemo(defaultContent), making it impossible for Contentstack Live Preview SDK updates to propagate to any of the 236+ consumers of useContent. Enabling live preview requires this provider to accept and react to live preview callbacks — the content must become updatable after initial render.
  - **Minimal fix:** Replace useMemo with useState so content can be updated after initial render, then add a useEffect that subscribes to the Contentstack Live Preview SDK's onLivePreview callback (which provides updated content on each CMS edit) and calls the setter. The provider currently freezes content at line 56-60; changing useMemo to useState(defaultContent) and wiring the live preview callback into a setState is the minimal change. The Contentstack SDK's live_preview config and onLivePreview handler must also be added wherever the Contentstack client is initialized (likely in getStaticProps or a server utility), but the provider is the causal site that must accept dynamic updates.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When a page is opened in Live Preview mode, the tester sees the draft (unpublished) content values displayed on the page — for example, the edited headline, body text, and image appear on screen instead of the previously published versions.
- While a page is open in Live Preview mode and a content editor saves a change in the CMS, the tester sees the page update to show the new values (e.g., the changed headline text) without the page reloading or the browser refreshing.
- When a page is opened outside of Live Preview mode, the tester sees the published content values displayed on the page — the published headline, body text, and images appear as they do on the live site today.
- When a page in Live Preview mode contains rich content, the tester sees it fully rendered: links appear as clickable links showing their link text and navigating to their destination, images display visibly with their alt text, and tables display with their rows and cell values visible.
- When a Live Preview session ends or loses its connection while the page is open, the tester sees the page continue to display the last shown content, and no error message or error screen appears.
- When displayed content includes sub-items (such as entries embedded within other entries), the tester sees every sub-item's values rendered on the page — each sub-item's title, text, and images are all visible in place.

